C = [
("h1", "1. How Paul Names Christ"),
("p", "Claim: Paul names Christ in more than one way, and the names are not interchangeable. Before any passage is read at length, the volume lists what Paul calls him, so that the reader can see how much of the work is done by a single word and how much by a whole sentence."),
("sub", "1.1 The names in Paul’s letters"),
("p", "The most common of Paul’s words for Christ are Lord, Christ and Son. Paul opens Romans with “Concerning his Son Jesus Christ our Lord” (Romans 1:3). He writes to Corinth of “one Lord Jesus Christ, by whom are all things, and we by him” (1 Corinthians 8:6). He ends the great hymn of Philippians with “that Jesus Christ is Lord” (Philippians 2:11). These are the ordinary names, and they appear in every letter."),
("p", "Beside them stand names of a different kind, which describe him and do not only title him. He is “the image of the invisible God” (Colossians 1:15). He is “the head of the body, the church” (Colossians 1:18). He is “the man Christ Jesus” (1 Timothy 2:5). He is the Saviour (Titus 2:13). Last, in a few places, a word for God is joined to him in the same sentence: “God was manifest in the flesh” (1 Timothy 3:16), “the great God and our Saviour Jesus Christ” (Titus 2:13), “who is over all, God blessed for ever” (Romans 9:5)."),
("sub", "1.2 What the names do not settle"),
("p", "A title alone does not state a nature. “Lord” in 1 Corinthians 8:5 is used of “lords many,” and “son” in Exodus 4:22 is said of Israel: “Israel is my son, even my firstborn.” The volume therefore does not rest on a title. It rests on the sentences in which Paul says what Christ did, what was done through him and what dwells in him. Those sentences are the subject of the chapters that follow."),
("ctbl", "Paul’s ways of naming Christ, and what each is set against.", [
    ("The saying", "Where", "What it is set against"),
    ("“Who, being in the form of God”", "Philippians 2:6", "A quarrel in the church, answered by his example"),
    ("“the image of the invisible God, the firstborn of every creature”", "Colossians 1:15", "Teachers of philosophy and the rudiments of the world"),
    ("“in him dwelleth all the fulness of the Godhead bodily”", "Colossians 2:9", "The same teachers; the claim that something is lacking in him"),
    ("“God was manifest in the flesh”", "1 Timothy 3:16", "How to behave in the house of God"),
    ("“the great God and our Saviour Jesus Christ”", "Titus 2:13", "The grace that teaches, and the blessed hope"),
    ("“who is over all, God blessed for ever”", "Romans 9:5", "The sorrow of Paul over his kinsmen the Israelites"),
    ("“the man Christ Jesus”", "1 Timothy 2:5", "One God, and one mediator between God and men"),
  ], [0.40, 0.20, 0.40]),
("sub", "1.3 The questions the volume asks"),
("p", "Of each passage the volume asks four things. Who is named, and by what words? What is said of him before the incarnation, in it, or apart from it? What problem is the saying set against? And what does the passage leave unsaid? The last question matters most. A passage that says Christ is “the image of the invisible God” does not, in that sentence, say how the image relates to the Father in a way a later council would state. The volume reports what is said and marks the point at which the sentence stops."),
("sub", "1.4 The order of the chapters"),
("p", "The chapters go in the order of the thesis. Chapters 2 to 4 take the hymn of Philippians. Chapters 5 to 8 take Colossians: the passage in its place, the image and the firstborn, the creating, and the fulness. Chapters 9 to 11 take the three shorter sayings, in Romans, 1 Timothy and Titus. Chapter 12 turns to the man, and chapter 13 to the Father and the Son. Chapters 14 to 16 gather the sayings by their order, by their problems, and by what they leave unsaid. Chapter 17 answers objections. A reader who wishes to test the thesis in the shortest way may read chapter 14, which sets the sayings in one table, and then turn back to the passage that raises a doubt."),
("sub", "1.5 What the volume takes from the chapter"),
("bul", [
    "Paul has ordinary names for Christ (Lord, Christ, Son) and descriptive names (image, head, the man).",
    "In a few sentences a word for God is joined to Christ without a title in between.",
    "A title alone does not settle a nature, and the volume rests on sentences, not on titles.",
    "Each passage is read for who is named, what is said, what problem it answers and what it leaves unsaid.",
  ]),
("p", "The next chapters take the passages in the order of the thesis, beginning with the hymn of Philippians 2, because it is the passage that asks most plainly what “in the form of God” can mean."),
]
